Hint 2: Avoid naively applying the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to \(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2\) alone, as the condition involves more complicated coefficients. After you substitute with \(y_i\), rewrite the sum such that you can represent the equation as \(\sum_{i=1}^n \left(\sum_{j=i}^n y_j\right)^2 = 1\). This telescoping structure is crucial for the correct next steps.
Hint 3: Do **not** ignore the nested structure that comes from the substitution. Define \(z_i = \sum_{j=i}^n y_j\), so the condition becomes \(\sum_{i=1}^n z_i^2 = 1\). The Cauchy-Schwarz inequality should then be applied to \(\sum_{i=1}^n (2i-1)z_i\), helping you relate this sum to the desired bound.
