Hint 2: Avoid mixing up the **quantity to maximize**; the sum is not simply the count of all houses minus one, nor is it maximized by making one very tall house and many short ones. Use **the principle of maximizing the number of 1-floor and 2-floor house combinations**, and express the sum as the number of ordered (short, tall) pairs.
Hint 3: The arrangement should balance the number of 1-floor and 2-floor houses to maximize the total number of shorter-taller pairs. Use the **product rule**, setting \( x \) as the number of 1-floor houses and \( y \) as the number of 2-floor houses, constrained by \( x + 2y = 30 \), and maximize \( x \times y \) under this constraint as an intermediate step.

