Hint 2: Avoid using the property that opposite angles in a quadrilateral sum to \(180^\circ\), since that only applies to cyclic figures, not tangential ones. Instead, recognize that the sum of all interior angles in a pentagon is \(540^\circ\) and, after subtracting the three given angles, set up the equation for the two unknown angles: \[ \text{Let } x = \angle BAD, \, y = \angle ADE;\; x + y = 540^\circ - 104^\circ - 104^\circ - 104^\circ. \]
