ITEM 85/180
HINT:
Hint 1: Do not assume that "mirroring" is exclusively an advantage for the first player. With two equal piles, the mirroring move actually benefits the second player, who can always respond to any move to restore symmetry. Use the symmetry strategy: after any first player move, have the second player take the same number of balls from the other pile to equalize them again.
Hint 2: Avoid thinking that the first player can always force a win by breaking symmetry on their first move. In two-pile Nim, the critical technique is to analyze the nim-sum (xor) of the pile sizes: when both are equal, the nim-sum is zero. Note that if the first player changes one pile, the second player can always mirror the move so both piles return to being equal after each second player turn.
Hint 3: Be careful not to assume that ending with both piles at one ball favors the first player. If each turn maintains both piles at equal count via the mirroring strategy, the second player will always be the one to make the last move. Try tracking the pile sizes after each pair of moves, noticing that after each round, the parity of the turn alternates who can take the final balls.
